The test cannot await startup reliably because `void main() async` hides its initialization `Future`. Expose an awaitable startup boundary, then await it before pumping.

If you can change the entrypoint signature, preserve the existing initialization order and change it to `Future<void>`:

```dart
Future<void> main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  await Firebase.initializeApp();
  await initializeDatabase(); // Your existing initialization.
  runApp(const MyApp());
}
```

Then update the integration test:

```dart
IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();

testWidgets('startup reaches the first screen', (tester) async {
  await app.main();
  await tester.pumpAndSettle();

  expect(find.byKey(const ValueKey('home_screen')), findsOneWidget);
});
```

Use your actual initialization calls and first-screen finder. If the app must retain a `void` entrypoint, extract the initialization and `runApp` sequence into a shared `Future<void> bootstrap()` and have the test await that instead.

Do not mechanically add `await` to every project: a synchronous `void main()` should still be called directly. If the first screen has continuous animations, use a bounded wait for the expected screen instead of `pumpAndSettle()`.

Cover initialization ordering and failure with injected or fake initializers: the app should mount only after initialization completes, and initialization errors should remain observable without mounting the app.

